690: a convex combination of increasing, proper fibre coordinates produces
691: an increasing proper coordinate agreeing with the prescribed one where
692: the outer partition has weight1. This is a relative product
693: trivialization. Its derivative over the fixed (x,y) base is an upper
694: triangular real matrix with identity base block and a positive fibre
695: derivative. Contracting the off-diagonal block and then the positive
696: scalar gives a homotopy to the identity relative to the prescribed
697: outer product. Thus this comparison retains the stable boundary
698: framing, not just the orbit diffeomorphism type. In particular one must not equate all quadratic
699: invariants in different normal metrics at a fixed point: their
700: transition can involve a radial square root. The metric choice and the
701: positive-coordinate construction avoid that error.
702: 
703: Apply this calculation to the square Mumford fan before triangulation.
704: The square model is the fibre product of the two nodal elliptic models;
705: the A2 triangulation is its small resolution, one per lattice cell.
706: The circle acts in the second nodal factor. Its orbit is a smooth
707: circle bundle over the base disc, since its local quotient at the node
708: is(q=uv,(|u|²-|v|²)/2). It is a product over the disc. Before resolving
709: the conifold, the total orbit is therefore E_I1 timesS1, where E_I1 is
710: a compact neighbourhood of one nodal elliptic fibre in a smooth
711: elliptic surface. The relative comparison above replaces the node
712: orbit without changing this product diffeomorphism type or its outer
713: boundary marking.
714: 
715: The bounded toric units of the actual construction do not change this
716: statement. Choose their logarithms on the cusp disc. Their interpolation
717: to constant units gives the same unimodular charts and a proper smooth
718: family after shrinking the disc uniformly; the logarithmic terms
719: dominate every bounded unit. Averaging an Ehresmann field gives an
720: equivariant comparison. In the source elliptic factor, the gamma deck
721: translates by the section6P. Its Tate coordinate is a unit a(q), and
722: the explicit path Z->exp(t log a(q)) Z extends across the nodal fibre.
723: Its suspension is consequently a product E_I1 timesS1_gamma. The
724: source Picard line restricted to an elliptic neighbourhood selected
725: disjoint from the fixed sphere is topologically trivial: its degree on
726: the nodal fibre is zero, and that neighbourhood retracts onto the nodal
727: fibre. An extending Hermitian metric removes its radial unit on this
728: slice. This statement does not trivialize the principal-circle line on
729: the complement of the fixed sphere in the entire orbit neighbourhood.
730: That line has Chern number one, up to the fixed orientation convention,
731: on a meridian two-sphere; its meridian class is retained in every
732: subsequent clutch or index comparison. These comparisons preserve the integral
733: gamma,u,w and q markings on the outer boundary. Changes in the affine
734: stable frames consist of unit scalings and triangular shears and are
735: homotopic to the indicated diagonal factors; no three-dimensional
736: Pontryagin framing class is introduced by a logarithm of a unit.
737: 
738: The fixed sphere projects in E_I1 to the normalization of the nodal
739: fibre. The small-resolution interval separates its two preimages of
740: the node in the gamma direction. Its projected normal complex line
741: has transition a'=z²a, namely O(-2). Its Euler number, with the complex
742: orientation of that line and of the normalization, is-2. The gamma
743: coordinate of this sphere can be confined to an arbitrarily short arc
744: by taking the relative node comparison sufficiently small. Rotation of
745: the S1_gamma factor is a free action on the cusp orbit body and extends
746: the marked boundary gamma circle action.
747: 
748: This proves the claimed product comparison with the explicit boundary
749: marking, rather than identifying the singular central fibre itself
